MEASURED in a throwaway git worktree at the staged bytes (d7fafa4f2d60), both files compiled clean by javac 25 (--release 25, no diagnostics), written under ingest/src/main/java/binjava/ingest/, staged, and scanned.

  // Probe1.java -- javax.sql sits beside the banned java.sql and is not listed
  public String hostOf(javax.sql.DataSource ds) throws Exception {
      try (var conn = ds.getConnection()) { return conn.getMetaData().getURL(); }
  }

  // Probe2.java -- JNDI opens a socket from a package nobody listed
  public Object lookup(String name) throws Exception {
      return new javax.naming.InitialContext().lookup(name);
  }

`printf ... | python3 scripts/io_seam_scan.py` printed `2` and NO violation lines; `./scripts/check-io-seam.sh --full` printed `ok 103 business-logic file(s) name none of the 26 banned constructs` and exited 0. `var` keeps the java.sql result type unnamed, so the java.sql ban never fires. Both open a real network connection from business logic.

The package half is therefore an ENUMERATION exactly like the clock half, not a closure. Three places in the same commit say so correctly -- io_seam_scan.py:150 ('the honest rule is that each PACKAGE a reach can live in must be named'), check-io-seam.sh:14-17, code-structure.md:36 ('It is a DENY-LIST and cannot be complete') -- and AGENTS.md:156-157 says it itself four lines earlier ('It does NOT close subclassing OUT of one'). AGENTS.md:167-168 then contradicts all four.

Why this is not cosmetic: AGENTS.md is layer 0, loaded into EVERY session by every agent tool, and it is the only one of the five documents most readers will ever see. A reader who believes 'closed by construction' stops looking for the next javax.* package, which is precisely the behaviour that let java.util.jar.JarFile survive to round 7. And the backlog cell (docs/internal/product/backlog.md:45) lists '"closed by construction", stated in four documents while java.io was a class-name prefix' among the FIVE OF MY OWN CLAIMS WERE FALSIFIED BY REVIEW AND CORRECTED HERE -- so the tree claims a correction it did not make. That is the defect class this task has now reproduced across seven rounds.

Also at AGENTS.md:158-159, a fragment that reads backwards: 'A subclass cannot escape the way it escapes a banned class name.' The intended sentence is io_seam_scan.py:105-106's 'A subclass cannot escape a banned PACKAGE the way it escapes a banned class' -- but placed immediately after 'It does NOT close subclassing OUT of one' it asserts the opposite of the sentence it follows.

Fix: delete 'closed by construction' at AGENTS.md:168 and say what the scripts say -- the package half ends a sibling hunt INSIDE a listed package and is still a hand-named list of packages; drop or repair the fragment at :158-159.

MEASURED at the staged bytes. This file, which reaches nothing beyond the stream it is handed, is refused twice:

  package binjava.ingest;
  import java.io.DataInputStream;
  import java.io.InputStream;
  import java.io.Serializable;
  class FR1 implements Serializable {
      long readLen(InputStream in) throws java.io.IOException {
          return new DataInputStream(in).readLong();
      }
  }

`python3 scripts/io_seam_scan.py` prints:
  /tmp/fr1.java:2: java.io reaches the filesystem directly
  /tmp/fr1.java:4: java.io reaches the filesystem directly

DataInputStream and DataOutputStream are pure wrappers over a stream the caller supplies -- they open nothing -- and Serializable is a marker interface. The header at :155-157 says the unused names present in ALLOWED are 'their obvious siblings, listed ahead of need so the first use of Flushable is not a spurious refusal'; for a project whose whole job is moving binary segments, DataInputStream and DataOutputStream are more obvious siblings than Flushable and are the pair an author will reach for first.

This is the failure mode the backlog cell names as the one worth avoiding -- 'a gate that refuses correct code is one an author edits rather than obeys'. Minor because nothing in the tree is refused today and the remedy is two lines in ALLOWED, each pinned by an accepting case like the rest. A backlog row is an acceptable resolution.
